# Europe batch (1948-2023): every brief rewritten to rules zero to four.
OVERRIDE = True
A = {
"d-berlinairlift": dict(
 bluf="Soviet leader Joseph Stalin cut West Berlin's road and rail links to force the Western powers out of the city or off their plan for a West German state. US and British air forces supplied the city's 2 million people by air for eleven months until Stalin lifted the blockade in May 1949. The West had held its position without a shot, West Germans became allies, and NATO and the Federal Republic followed within the year.",
 now="The airlift won West Berlin without a shot, turned West Germans from enemies into allies, and produced NATO and the Federal Republic within a year; it fixed the rule that the West would hold its positions in Europe.",
 story="The Western powers introduced the Deutsche Mark in their zones in June 1948. Soviet leader Joseph Stalin answered by cutting the road and rail links to West Berlin. US and British air forces flew in food and coal, at the peak landing every few minutes at Tempelhof. Stalin lifted the blockade in May 1949.",
 why="The new currency was a step toward a separate West German state. Stalin wanted the Western powers out of Berlin or back at the table on his terms.",
 trig="The currency reform of 20 June 1948.",
 then="NATO was founded in April 1949. Germany split into two states that year, the Federal Republic in the west and the German Democratic Republic in the east. Berlin stayed divided under four-power occupation until 1990.",
 fact="Pilots dropped sweets on small parachutes to Berlin children and the planes were called 'raisin bombers'; Tempelhof is now a public park."),
"na194870": dict(
 bluf="US Secretary of State George Marshall offered American money to rebuild Western Europe's economies, keep communist parties out of power and create markets for US goods. About $13 billion flowed between 1948 and 1952, on condition that recipients planned its use together. Western Europe recovered, tied itself to the United States and began the coordination that grew into the European Community; the Soviet bloc refused the aid, and the economic division of Europe hardened.",
 now="The aid tied Western Europe's recovery to the United States and required the recipients to plan together, which produced the Organisation for European Economic Co-operation in 1948 and the habit of coordination behind the Coal and Steel Community of 1951.",
 story="Western Europe in 1947 was short of food, coal and dollars, and communist parties were strong in France and Italy. US Secretary of State George Marshall proposed American aid in a speech at Harvard in June 1947. The United States gave about $13 billion (well over $150 billion today) between 1948 and 1952 to rebuild factories, farms and trade. Soviet leader Joseph Stalin refused the offer and made Czechoslovakia and Poland refuse it.",
 why="The United States wanted a Western Europe prosperous enough to buy American goods and resist communism. Europe's dollar shortage made recovery without aid slow.",
 then="Western European output passed its prewar level in the early 1950s. The Soviet bloc answered with Comecon in 1949. The coordination the aid required produced the OEEC and the habits behind the European Coal and Steel Community of 1951.",
 fact="George Marshall won the Nobel Peace Prize for it in 1953, the first professional soldier to do so."),
"eu194999": dict(
 bluf="The United States, Canada and ten Western European states bound themselves to treat an armed attack on one as an attack on all, to deter Soviet expansion and to commit American power to Europe's defence in advance. The alliance held through the Cold War without fighting it. After 1991 it expanded east; Russia cites the expansion as a grievance, and the new members call it the protection they joined for.",
 now="The guarantee is the reason Western Europe has faced no attack since 1949 and the reason each eastern enlargement, from 1999 to Finland and Sweden in 2023-24, became a dispute with Moscow.",
 story="The Soviet blockade of Berlin was in its tenth month. Twelve countries signed the North Atlantic Treaty in Washington on 4 April 1949. Its Article 5 committed members to treat an armed attack on one as an attack on all. It was the first peacetime alliance the United States had joined since 1778.",
 why="The communist coup in Czechoslovakia in February 1948 and the Berlin Blockade convinced Western governments that the Soviet Union would push wherever it met no force. Western Europe could deter it only with the United States committed beforehand.",
 then="West Germany joined in 1955 and the Soviet Union answered with the Warsaw Pact the same month. After the Cold War NATO admitted former Soviet-bloc states from 1999, bombed Serbia in 1999 and fought in Afghanistan from 2001. Finland joined in 2023 and Sweden in 2024.",
 fact="Article 5 has been invoked once, by the United States after the attacks of 11 September 2001."),
"eu194999b": dict(
 bluf="The Western Allies set up a West German state to anchor their zones in the Western system; the Soviet Union answered with an East German state in its zone. Each got the state it wanted and gave up any chance of a united, neutral Germany. Germany became the front line of the Cold War, and the two states served as showcases for rival systems until reunification in 1990.",
 now="Reunification in 1990 joined two societies that had run on rival systems for 41 years; the gaps in wages, wealth and voting between east and west persist and shape German politics.",
 story="The wartime Allies had failed to agree on Germany's future, and the Berlin Blockade of 1948-49 ended the attempt. The Federal Republic of Germany was founded in the Western zones in May 1949, with Bonn as its capital; Christian Democrat leader Konrad Adenauer became chancellor in September. In October the Soviet zone became the German Democratic Republic, with Socialist Unity Party leader Walter Ulbricht as its dominant figure.",
 why="The Western powers wanted their zones rebuilt and inside their system. The Soviet Union wanted its zone kept and would accept a united Germany only on terms the West refused.",
 then="West Germany joined NATO in 1955 and East Germany the Warsaw Pact. About 3 million East Germans fled west before the Berlin Wall closed the border in 1961.",
 fact="Bonn, a university town of about 100,000, was chosen as a 'provisional' capital and kept the role for 41 years."),
"eu1953100": dict(
 bluf="Soviet leader Joseph Stalin died without a designated successor, and the men around him wanted to keep the system and end the terror that had threatened each of them. Nikita Khrushchev won the succession struggle, denounced Stalin's crimes in 1956 and released millions from the Gulag. The system proved able to reform within limits, and the limits were shown in Hungary that same year.",
 now="Khrushchev's denunciation freed the Gulag's prisoners and ended mass terror while leaving the party's monopoly intact; how far the system could reform without collapsing was the open question from 1956 to 1991. Stalin's partial rehabilitation under Russian President Vladimir Putin is part of a wider rewriting of Soviet history.",
 story="Soviet leader Joseph Stalin suffered a stroke at his dacha outside Moscow on 1 March 1953 and died four days later, aged 74; his guards, afraid of disturbing him, had delayed calling doctors. Secret police chief Lavrentiy Beria, Premier Georgy Malenkov and party secretary Nikita Khrushchev shared power at first. Khrushchev emerged as leader, and in February 1956 he denounced Stalin's crimes to the 20th Party Congress in a 'secret speech'.",
 why="Stalin died of a stroke at 74 without a designated successor. His guards' fear of disturbing him delayed medical help by hours.",
 then="Beria was arrested in June 1953 and shot in December. Millions were released from the Gulag. Khrushchev opened a cautious 'thaw' in culture and foreign policy and crushed the Hungarian rising in November 1956.",
 fact="Stalin's own physicians were in prison when he collapsed, accused in the 'doctors' plot' announced that January."),
"eu1956101": dict(
 bluf="Hungarians rose to end Soviet control, and reform communist Imre Nagy as prime minister promised free elections and neutrality. Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev sent in tanks and crushed the rising in a week, at a cost of about 2,500 Hungarian dead and 200,000 refugees. Moscow had shown it would use force to hold its bloc, the West had shown it would stay out, and the division of Europe held on those terms until 1989.",
 now="The crushing of the uprising showed that the West would not intervene behind the Iron Curtain and that Moscow would use force to hold its bloc; the rule held until 1989.",
 story="On 23 October 1956 a student demonstration in Budapest grew into a nationwide uprising. Reform communist Imre Nagy became prime minister, promised free elections and, on 1 November, announced that Hungary would leave the Warsaw Pact and become neutral. On 4 November Soviet tanks entered Budapest and crushed the revolt; about 2,500 Hungarians were killed and 200,000 fled. Nagy was tried in secret and hanged in 1958.",
 why="Soviet control itself was the grievance. Khrushchev's denunciation of Stalin in February had loosened the fear that held the bloc together, and Poland had won a partial relaxation in October without invasion.",
 then="The West, divided by the Suez crisis the same week, confined itself to protest. Moscow installed János Kádár, who had Nagy executed and later allowed a softer 'goulash communism'. Western communist parties lost members over the invasion.",
 fact="Hungary marks 23 October as a national holiday."),
"eu1957102": dict(
 bluf="France, West Germany, Italy and the Benelux countries set out to tie their economies together so that war among them became impractical and to give Europe weight between the superpowers. The treaty created a common market with a customs union and shared institutions. It grew into the European Union of 27 states, the furthest any group of states has gone in pooling sovereignty.",
 now="The six founders and the states that joined them have fought no war among themselves since 1957; the euro crisis of 2010 and Brexit in 2016 showed where pooled sovereignty strains.",
 story="The European Coal and Steel Community of 1951 had pooled the six countries' war industries. On 25 March 1957 France, West Germany, Italy, Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxembourg signed the treaty in Rome creating the European Economic Community, with a common market, a customs union and a commission in Brussels. It came into force on 1 January 1958.",
 why="The founders wanted war between France and Germany made impractical by shared industry and trade. They also wanted a market large enough to compete with the United States and a voice between the superpowers.",
 then="Britain joined in 1973. The Maastricht Treaty of 1992 created the European Union and set the path to a single currency. The Union had 27 members after Britain's departure in 2020.",
 fact="The Commission the treaty created had nine members; today's has 27, one per member state."),
"na195774": dict(
 bluf="The Soviet space programme under chief designer Sergei Korolev launched the first artificial satellite to prove the R-7 missile and to beat the United States into orbit. It succeeded. Americans read it as proof that Soviet rockets could reach them with nuclear warheads, and the United States answered with NASA, a surge in science funding and the research agency whose work later produced the internet.",
 now="The space race the launch set off produced the satellites that carry GPS, television and weather forecasting, and the research funding, through NASA and DARPA, from which the internet grew.",
 story="On 4 October 1957 the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1, a 58 cm metal sphere that beeped radio signals as it circled the Earth every 96 minutes. The launcher was the R-7, the first intercontinental ballistic missile, built by chief designer Sergei Korolev's team. Americans grasped that a rocket able to orbit a satellite could carry a nuclear warhead across the world.",
 why="The Soviets had built the R-7 and could prove it with a satellite. Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev wanted the prestige of beating the United States.",
 then="The United States created NASA in 1958 and passed the National Defense Education Act the same year. The Advanced Research Projects Agency, also founded in 1958, funded the work that led to the internet.",
 fact="Sputnik was a polished ball that only beeped, and amateur radio operators around the world could hear it pass overhead."),
"d-gagarin": dict(
 bluf="The Soviet programme under chief designer Sergei Korolev put cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin into orbit to claim the first human spaceflight and the prestige that went with it. He circled the Earth once in 108 minutes and landed safely. The United States answered within six weeks with President John F. Kennedy's commitment to land on the Moon, and the space race became a central contest of the Cold War.",
 now="Gagarin's flight made manned spaceflight the measure of a superpower; the contest it started is run again today between the United States and China, with the Moon once more the goal.",
 story="On 12 April 1961 cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin's Vostok 1 lifted off from Baikonur and circled the Earth once in 108 minutes. He ejected during the descent, as planned, and parachuted down near Saratov. He was 27.",
 why="The Soviet Union had a lead in heavy rockets from its missile programme. Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev wanted Cold War prestige.",
 then="On 25 May 1961 US President John F. Kennedy committed the United States to landing a man on the Moon before the decade ended. The space race became a central front of the Cold War. Gagarin died in a training flight in 1968.",
 fact="Soviet officials concealed for years that Gagarin had parachuted, because the record rules required a pilot to land with his craft."),
"eu1961103": dict(
 bluf="East German leader Walter Ulbricht, with Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev's consent, sealed the border in Berlin to stop an exodus that had taken about 3 million people west and threatened the state's survival. The wall stopped the flight. The communist state stabilised behind it and became known as the regime that walled in its own people, and Berlin stayed the front line of the Cold War until the wall opened in 1989.",
 now="The wall stopped the drain of skilled workers and let East Germany survive 28 more years; its opening on 9 November 1989 marked the end of the Cold War in Europe.",
 story="About 3 million East Germans had fled west through Berlin since 1949, many of them young and skilled. In the early hours of 13 August 1961 East German police and workers strung barbed wire along the sector border, with Soviet approval. Concrete followed within days.",
 why="The exodus was draining the state's workforce. Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev wanted the Berlin crisis closed without war.",
 then="At least 140 people died trying to cross. Berlin became the front line of the Cold War, and the wall stood until 9 November 1989.",
 fact="East German leader Walter Ulbricht told a press conference on 15 June 1961 that nobody intended to build a wall; the wire went up two months later."),
"d-testban": dict(
 bluf="The United States, the Soviet Union and Britain agreed to stop testing nuclear weapons in the atmosphere, under water and in space, to cut the fallout the public feared and to step back after the Cuban missile crisis. The treaty ended their atmospheric tests. It was the first major arms-control agreement and opened the process that led to the Non-Proliferation Treaty of 1968.",
 now="More than 120 countries are parties to it, and it began the arms-control process that keeps nuclear testing rare today.",
 story="Hundreds of atmospheric tests had spread radioactive strontium-90 around the world and into milk. Negotiators for US President John F. Kennedy, Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev and British Prime Minister Harold Macmillan met in Moscow in July 1963. The treaty was signed there on 5 August 1963 and banned all tests except those underground.",
 why="Public fear of fallout in milk and food came first. The missile crisis of October 1962 had made both leaders want a step back.",
 then="France and China stayed outside the treaty and kept testing in the atmosphere into the 1970s. The Non-Proliferation Treaty followed in 1968.",
 fact="The treaty text was negotiated in ten days."),
"eu1968104": dict(
 bluf="Czechoslovak party leader Alexander Dubček set out to reform communism from within, with open debate and an end to censorship; Soviet leader Leonid Brezhnev sent the Warsaw Pact's armies to stop it. About 250,000 troops ended the experiment in a night. Moscow then declared a right to intervene in any socialist country, and reform in the bloc stayed off the table until Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev dropped that doctrine in 1989.",
 now="Many Western communists broke with Moscow after the invasion, and its logic, that Moscow will not let a neighbour drift west, resurfaced in Georgia in 2008 and Ukraine from 2014.",
 story="From January 1968 Czechoslovak Communist Party leader Alexander Dubček eased censorship and allowed open debate, 'socialism with a human face'. On the night of 20-21 August about 250,000 Warsaw Pact troops from the Soviet Union, Poland, Hungary, Bulgaria and East Germany entered the country. The government ordered no resistance. Dubček was flown to Moscow and signed a protocol reversing the reforms.",
 why="Moscow feared the reforms would spread to Poland and Ukraine and pull Czechoslovakia out of the bloc. Dubček had kept the party in control of everything except the press.",
 then="Brezhnev declared in November 1968 that the Soviet Union would intervene wherever socialism was threatened, the 'Brezhnev Doctrine'. Dubček was removed in April 1969 and 'normalisation' purged the party. Student Jan Palach burned himself to death in protest in January 1969.",
 fact="Dubček ended his career as a forestry official and returned as speaker of parliament after the Velvet Revolution in 1989."),
"eu1968104b": dict(
 bluf="Students in Paris wanted a freer university and a freer society; the workers who joined them wanted higher wages and a say at work. Together they produced a strike of perhaps 10 million, the largest in French history, and forced President Charles de Gaulle to call elections, which he won. The institutions held, and the liberalisation of law and custom the protesters demanded arrived over the following decade.",
 now="The protests brought France to a general strike and de Gaulle's departure within a year; the social liberalisation they demanded arrived in the following decade, in law and in custom.",
 story="Clashes between students and police in the Latin Quarter in early May 1968 spread to the factories. A general strike of perhaps 10 million workers followed, the largest in French history. President Charles de Gaulle flew to a French base in Germany on 29 May, returned, dissolved the National Assembly and won a landslide in June.",
 why="An overcrowded, authoritarian university system came first. A conservative society under a ten-year presidency and the worldwide student mood of 1968 supplied the rest.",
 then="The Grenelle agreements raised the minimum wage by about a third. Universities were reformed. De Gaulle resigned in April 1969 after losing a referendum.",
 fact="The slogan 'under the paving stones, the beach' came from the cobbles students pulled up for barricades."),
"eu1975105": dict(
 bluf="Soviet leader Leonid Brezhnev wanted Western recognition of Europe's postwar borders; the United States and its allies wanted eased tensions and traded recognition for commitments on human rights and freedom of movement. Both got what they asked for. Dissidents across the bloc used the human-rights clauses against their own governments, which eroded communist legitimacy over the following 14 years.",
 now="The human-rights commitments gave dissidents from Moscow to Prague a text their governments had signed, and the follow-up conferences a forum to cite it; the OSCE that grew from the process monitors elections and conflicts in Europe today.",
 story="Thirty-five states, including the United States, the Soviet Union and Canada, signed the Final Act in Helsinki on 1 August 1975. The Soviet Union won recognition of its postwar borders. In return it signed commitments to human rights and freedom of movement. Dissident groups, Charter 77 in Czechoslovakia and the Moscow Helsinki Group, cited the promises against their governments.",
 why="Détente came first: Brezhnev wanted the borders of 1945 recognised and the West wanted lower tensions. Western Europeans insisted on the human-rights basket as the price.",
 then="The Moscow Helsinki Group formed in 1976 and Charter 77 in 1977. Violations were aired at follow-up conferences in Belgrade and Madrid. The process became the OSCE in 1995.",
 fact="The Final Act is a political declaration rather than a treaty and bound nobody in law; the dissidents made it bind."),
"eu1975105b": dict(
 bluf="Spanish dictator Francisco Franco named Prince Juan Carlos his successor to carry his regime past his death. King Juan Carlos I instead backed reformers who legalised parties, held free elections in 1977 and wrote a democratic constitution in 1978. Spain moved from dictatorship to democracy by agreement among former enemies, and its method became the model for transitions in Latin America and Eastern Europe.",
 now="Spain's negotiated transition became the model for later exits from dictatorship; its 'pact of forgetting' about civil-war crimes remains contested, and a 2022 law on democratic memory reopened it.",
 story="Spanish dictator Francisco Franco had ruled since winning the civil war in 1939. He died on 20 November 1975, and Prince Juan Carlos, whom he had named successor, became king two days later. King Juan Carlos I appointed Adolfo Suárez prime minister in 1976; Suárez legalised parties, including the communists, and held free elections in June 1977. A democratic constitution was approved by referendum in December 1978.",
 why="Franco's death removed the man the regime rested on. Growth in the 1960s had produced a middle class ready for change, and the king and Suárez chose reform over confrontation.",
 then="An attempted military coup on 23 February 1981 failed when the king went on television to oppose it. Spain joined NATO in 1982 and the European Community in 1986.",
 fact="Prime Minister Carlos Arias Navarro wept on television as he announced Franco's death to the nation."),
"eu1980106": dict(
 bluf="Shipyard workers in Gdańsk led by electrician Lech Wałęsa struck for the right to an independent union, and a government short of money and afraid of a general strike conceded it. Solidarity grew to about 10 million members, the first independent union in the Soviet bloc. Martial law crushed it in 1981; it survived underground, won Poland's semi-free elections in 1989 and set off the collapse of communist rule across Eastern Europe.",
 now="Solidarity's survival underground and its victory in the elections of June 1989 gave the bloc its first non-communist government and the method, negotiation at a round table, that Hungary and Czechoslovakia followed within months.",
 story="Food price rises in July 1980 set off strikes across Poland. In August strikes at the Lenin Shipyard in Gdańsk, led by electrician Lech Wałęsa, spread across the country. On 31 August the government signed the Gdańsk Agreement recognising Solidarity, the first independent trade union in the Soviet bloc; it grew to about 10 million members. General Wojciech Jaruzelski declared martial law on 13 December 1981 and interned its leaders.",
 why="Rising food prices and a failing economy came first. The election of Polish Pope John Paul II in 1978 and his visit in 1979 had emboldened society, and anger at communist rule was general.",
 then="Martial law drove the union underground. Round-table talks in 1989 produced semi-free elections on 4 June 1989, which Solidarity swept; Tadeusz Mazowiecki became the bloc's first non-communist prime minister in August. Wałęsa was president from 1990 to 1995.",
 fact="Wałęsa signed the Gdańsk Agreement with an outsized souvenir pen bearing the Pope's picture."),
"eu1985107": dict(
 bluf="The Politburo chose Mikhail Gorbachev to revive a stagnant economy and a rigid state. His reforms, glasnost and perestroika, loosened the controls that held the system together, and his refusal to use force let Eastern Europe leave. Within six years the Cold War had ended peacefully, communism in Europe had collapsed and the Soviet Union had dissolved, the reverse of what he had set out to do.",
 now="Gorbachev is admired in the West and widely blamed in Russia, where Russian President Vladimir Putin's view of the Soviet collapse as a catastrophe shapes policy toward the former republics.",
 story="Three Soviet leaders had died in office between 1982 and 1985. On 11 March 1985 the Politburo chose Mikhail Gorbachev, aged 54, as general secretary. He introduced glasnost, openness, and perestroika, restructuring, to revive the economy and society. He withdrew from Afghanistan by 1989, signed the INF Treaty with US President Ronald Reagan in 1987 and declined to use force to hold Eastern Europe.",
 why="The Soviet economy was stagnating and the arms race was a burden it could no longer carry. The old leaders had died one after another, and the Politburo chose a younger man.",
 then="Communist governments fell across Eastern Europe in 1989. Germany reunified in October 1990. The Soviet Union dissolved in December 1991 and Gorbachev resigned.",
 fact="Gorbachev won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1990; in Russia's 1996 presidential election he took about 0.5 per cent of the vote."),
"d-reykjavik": dict(
 bluf="Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev wanted deep cuts in nuclear arms to relieve his economy; US President Ronald Reagan wanted the nuclear threat ended and his missile shield kept. For two days they came close to agreeing to abolish all nuclear weapons, and the talks broke over the shield. The trust built in the failure produced the INF Treaty a year later and the START treaty in 1991.",
 now="US President Ronald Reagan and Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev both later called it a turning point in the Cold War, and missile defence remains the main obstacle in arms talks with Russia.",
 story="US President Ronald Reagan and Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev met at Höfði House in Reykjavík on 11-12 October 1986. They discussed eliminating all ballistic missiles and then all nuclear weapons within ten years. The talks collapsed over Reagan's refusal to confine his Strategic Defense Initiative, the 'Star Wars' missile shield, to the laboratory.",
 why="Gorbachev wanted to cut military spending. Reagan wanted to end the nuclear threat and believed the shield would help.",
 then="The INF Treaty of December 1987 eliminated both sides' intermediate-range missiles. The START treaty of July 1991 cut long-range weapons.",
 fact="Reagan's offer to eliminate all ballistic missiles alarmed his own allies, whose defence rested on them."),
"eu1986108": dict(
 bluf="Operators at Chernobyl's Reactor 4 ran a safety test on a reactor with a known design flaw, with its safety systems disabled. The reactor exploded, its graphite core burned for days, and fallout spread across Belarus, Ukraine and Europe; about 30 workers and firefighters died within months and about 350,000 people were evacuated. The cover-up exposed the Soviet state's secrecy, pushed Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev's glasnost forward and set back nuclear power worldwide.",
 now="The accident froze nuclear construction across much of Europe for two decades and gave glasnost its first test; Russian forces occupied the site for five weeks in 2022.",
 story="In the early hours of 26 April 1986 operators running a safety test on Reactor 4 at Chernobyl, in Soviet Ukraine, triggered a power surge that blew the reactor apart and set its graphite core on fire. Two workers died in the blast and about 30 firefighters and staff within months from radiation. Authorities evacuated nearby Pripyat only the next day and admitted the accident only after Swedish monitors detected the fallout. Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev spoke about it on television 18 days later.",
 why="The RBMK reactor had a design flaw that made it unstable at low power. Operators running the test disabled safety systems and broke operating rules.",
 then="About 350,000 people were evacuated, and a 30 km exclusion zone remains. Fallout spread across Belarus, Ukraine and much of Europe. Gorbachev later called the accident a turning point that pushed glasnost forward.",
 fact="The 30 km exclusion zone has become an accidental wildlife refuge with wolves, bison and wild horses."),
"d-romania1989": dict(
 bluf="Romanians rose against dictator Nicolae Ceaușescu after years of hunger and repression, and the army joined them. Ceaușescu fled, was tried by a hasty military court and shot with his wife on Christmas Day; about 1,100 people died, the only violent overthrow of a communist regime in 1989. Former communists under Ion Iliescu seized the transition and held power until 1996, which slowed Romania's reforms.",
 now="Because former communists captured the transition, Romania reformed later and slower than its neighbours and joined the EU only in 2007; the hasty trial remains the core of the argument over whether the revolution was also a coup.",
 story="Protests began in Timișoara on 16 December 1989 over the eviction of a Hungarian pastor, László Tőkés. On 21 December crowds jeered Romanian dictator Nicolae Ceaușescu during a speech in Bucharest; the next day he and his wife Elena fled by helicopter as the army joined the revolt. They were tried by a hasty military court and shot on 25 December. About 1,100 people died in the fighting.",
 why="Years of hunger and repression under a cult of personality came first. Communist regimes had fallen next door within weeks, and the army chose the crowd.",
 then="Former communists led by Ion Iliescu took power as the National Salvation Front and held it until 1996. In June 1990 Iliescu brought miners to Bucharest to beat opposition protesters. Romania joined NATO in 2004 and the EU in 2007.",
 fact="Most of the dead were killed after Ceaușescu fled, in confused fighting with 'terrorists' who were never identified."),
"d-velvet": dict(
 bluf="Czechoslovak students and citizens, with playwright Václav Havel's Civic Forum speaking for them, set out to end communist rule once Soviet backing was gone. Mass protest and a general strike brought the government down in six weeks without a death. Havel was president by the end of the year, and the country became the model of a nonviolent transition.",
 now="The revolution showed that once Moscow withdrew its guarantee, a communist government with no will to shoot fell in weeks; Czechoslovakia's peaceful split in 1993 kept the same pattern.",
 story="Police beat student marchers in Prague on 17 November 1989. Protests grew to hundreds of thousands in Wenceslas Square, and a two-hour general strike followed on 27 November. The communist government gave up its monopoly on power, and playwright Václav Havel, leader of Civic Forum, became president on 29 December.",
 why="The Berlin Wall had opened eight days before. Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev had ruled out intervention, and the police violence of 17 November turned a student march into a national movement.",
 then="Free elections in June 1990 gave Civic Forum a majority. Czechoslovakia split peacefully into the Czech Republic and Slovakia on 1 January 1993.",
 fact="Crowds in Wenceslas Square jingled their keys to tell the regime it was time to go."),
"eu1989109": dict(
 bluf="East Germany's government, losing its citizens through Hungary and facing weekly marches in Leipzig, tried to ease the pressure with new travel rules. A botched announcement sent crowds to the checkpoints, and the guards opened the gates. The division of Germany ended in practice that night, communist governments fell across Eastern Europe within weeks, and Germany was reunified within a year.",
 now="Germany's reunification and NATO's later enlargement follow from that night; Russia holds that the West promised in 1990 that NATO would stay put, and Western governments hold that no such pledge was written down, an argument that runs to the war in Ukraine.",
 story="Through 1989 East Germans fled west via Hungary, which had opened its Austrian border, and marched every Monday in Leipzig. On 9 November the government loosened travel rules, and at a press conference Politburo spokesman Günter Schabowski, misreading his note, said they applied 'immediately'. Crowds gathered at the checkpoints, and at Bornholmer Strasse overwhelmed border guards opened the gates.",
 why="Economic stagnation and mass emigration came first. Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev refused to send Soviet troops to prop up the regime, and East German leader Erich Honecker had been removed three weeks earlier.",
 trig="The botched announcement of new travel rules on the evening of 9 November 1989.",
 then="Communist governments fell across Eastern Europe within weeks; Germany reunified on 3 October 1990, and the Soviet Union dissolved in December 1991.",
 fact="The first checkpoint, at Bornholmer Strasse, was opened around 11.30 pm by border officer Harald Jäger, who had been given no orders."),
"eu1990110": dict(
 bluf="West German Chancellor Helmut Kohl set out to absorb a collapsing East Germany into the Federal Republic before Moscow changed its mind, and to keep the whole inside NATO. He got both, with the consent of the four wartime Allies and a payment to the Soviet Union. Europe's largest economy and state took shape at a cost of well over a trillion euros for the east, with gaps in wages and politics that persist.",
 now="Gaps in wages and politics between east and west persist; the far-right AfD's strength in the east reflects those divides.",
 story="After the Berlin Wall opened, East Germans voted in March 1990 for parties promising quick unity, and a monetary union on 1 July brought them the West German mark. West German Chancellor Helmut Kohl negotiated the 'Two Plus Four' treaty with the four wartime victors: united Germany would stay in NATO, accept its border with Poland and cap its army, and Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev agreed in return for German money. East Germany joined the Federal Republic on 3 October 1990.",
 why="East Germany's government collapsed after the Wall opened, and East Germans voted for rapid unity. Kohl moved fast because Gorbachev's position in Moscow was weak.",
 then="Germany became Europe's largest economy and leading power. Rebuilding the east cost well over a trillion euros, and unemployment there soared as its industry closed.",
 fact="The Two Plus Four treaty capped the German armed forces at 370,000."),
"d-coup1991": dict(
 bluf="Soviet hardliners seized power to stop a Union Treaty that would have handed the republics far more power and to restore central control. The coup collapsed in three days when crowds defended Russia's parliament, Russian President Boris Yeltsin defied the plotters and troops refused to attack. The plotters destroyed the centre they had tried to save, Yeltsin became the dominant figure and the Soviet Union dissolved within four months.",
 now="The coup's failure showed the Soviet state could no longer command obedience, and Yeltsin's victory began a decade of chaotic reform in Russia that shaped the rise of Russian President Vladimir Putin.",
 story="On 19 August 1991 a committee of communist hardliners, including the KGB chief and the defence minister, declared a state of emergency and held Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev at his Crimean dacha, a day before the Union Treaty was to be signed. Crowds defended Russia's parliament, the White House; Russian President Boris Yeltsin denounced the coup from atop a tank. Troops refused to attack, and the plotters gave up on 21 August.",
 why="The planned Union Treaty would have handed the republics far more power. The plotters wanted the party, the KGB and the army back in control.",
 then="Yeltsin suspended the Communist Party in Russia. Republics declared independence one after another, and the Soviet Union dissolved in December 1991.",
 fact="Three young men killed under armoured vehicles on the Garden Ring on the night of 20-21 August were the coup's only deaths in Moscow."),
"eu1991111": dict(
 bluf="Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev tried to hold the Union together in a looser form; the leaders of Russia, Ukraine and Belarus, led by Russian President Boris Yeltsin, chose to end it. Fifteen states became independent and Russia lost an empire. The Cold War ended with the United States the sole superpower, and Russia's sense of loss became the material of its politics, which Russian President Vladimir Putin draws on in the war in Ukraine.",
 now="Ukraine, the Baltic states and the Central Asian states became independent. Russian President Vladimir Putin has called the collapse 'the greatest geopolitical catastrophe' of the century, and the war in Ukraine is partly a struggle over its legacy.",
 story="A failed hard-line coup in August 1991 destroyed Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev's authority. Ukraine voted for independence by more than 90 per cent on 1 December. On 8 December the leaders of Russia, Ukraine and Belarus, meeting at Belavezha, declared that the Union had ceased to exist, and eleven republics formed a Commonwealth of Independent States at Alma-Ata on 21 December. Gorbachev resigned on 25 December.",
 why="Gorbachev's reforms had loosened the controls that held the system together. Nationalism in the republics, a failing economy and the loss of Eastern Europe in 1989 did the rest.",
 trig="The failed hardliners' coup of August 1991.",
 then="Russia's economy collapsed in the 1990s amid 'shock therapy', hyperinflation and the rise of oligarchs. Vladimir Putin rose to power in 1999-2000.",
 fact="The Soviet flag came down over the Kremlin on the evening of 25 December 1991 and the Russian tricolour went up in its place."),
"eu1991111b": dict(
 bluf="Slovenia and Croatia declared independence to leave a Yugoslavia that Serbian President Slobodan Milošević was bending to Serbian ends; Milošević and the Yugoslav army fought to keep Serb-populated territory in one state. The wars that followed over the decade killed about 130,000-140,000 people and brought the Srebrenica genocide. Europe's worst fighting since 1945 ended only through NATO's first combat operations, and the disputes over Bosnia and Kosovo remain open.",
 now="The UN tribunal at The Hague convicted leaders of war crimes and genocide; the settlements in Bosnia and Kosovo remain contested and keep both outside the EU.",
 story="Slovenia and Croatia declared independence on 25 June 1991. The Yugoslav army fought a ten-day war in Slovenia and withdrew. In Croatia, Serb-dominated Yugoslav forces and local Serb militias fought a long war; Vukovar fell in November 1991. Bosnia's war from April 1992 brought the siege of Sarajevo, 'ethnic cleansing' and genocide.",
 why="Communism's collapse removed the party that had held the federation together. Serbian President Slobodan Milošević and Croatian President Franjo Tuđman mobilised ethnic fears, and the Yugoslav army sided with Serbia.",
 then="About 130,000-140,000 people died and millions were displaced. NATO bombing helped end the wars in Bosnia in 1995 and Kosovo in 1999. Milošević died in 2006 while on trial at The Hague.",
 fact="The siege of Sarajevo lasted 1,425 days, the longest siege of a capital in modern warfare."),
"eu1992112": dict(
 bluf="The European Community's leaders, with French President François Mitterrand and German Chancellor Helmut Kohl at the front, wanted a reunified Germany bound into Europe and integration deepened after the Cold War. The treaty created the European Union, EU citizenship, cooperation on foreign policy and a timetable for a single currency. It delivered the euro in 1999 and set off lasting arguments over sovereignty, from close referendums in Denmark and France to Brexit.",
 now="The treaty made the EU a political union with its own currency, which bound members tighter than a market and gave the euro crisis of 2010 and Brexit in 2016 their stakes.",
 story="Signed in Maastricht on 7 February 1992 and in force from 1 November 1993, the treaty turned the European Community into the European Union. It created EU citizenship, cooperation on foreign policy and justice, and a timetable and rules for a single currency. Danish voters rejected it in June 1992 and approved it with opt-outs in 1993; French voters approved it in September 1992 by 51 per cent.",
 why="European leaders wanted to deepen integration after the Cold War. French President François Mitterrand wanted a reunified Germany anchored in Europe, and German Chancellor Helmut Kohl accepted a shared currency as the price.",
 then="The euro launched in 1999 and entered circulation in 2002. The EU expanded east in 2004.",
 fact="Britain negotiated opt-outs from the single currency and the social chapter."),
"eu1995113": dict(
 bluf="Bosnian Serb commander Ratko Mladić set out to remove Bosniaks from eastern Bosnia and take the UN 'safe area' of Srebrenica. His forces overran it and killed more than 8,000 men and boys, Europe's worst atrocity since 1945. The failure of UN peacekeeping pushed NATO to intervene and the United States to broker the Dayton Accords, and international courts ruled the killings genocide.",
 now="International courts ruled it genocide, and Mladić and Bosnian Serb leader Radovan Karadžić were jailed for life; denial remains common in Serb nationalist politics.",
 story="In July 1995 Bosnian Serb forces under General Ratko Mladić overran the UN 'safe area' of Srebrenica, defended by lightly armed Dutch peacekeepers. Over the following days they separated Bosniak men and boys from the women and killed more than 8,000 of them. The bodies were buried in mass graves and later moved to hide them.",
 why="Mladić's forces aimed to remove Bosniaks from eastern Bosnia to create an ethnically Serb territory. The UN peacekeepers were too few and too lightly armed to stop them, and air support was refused.",
 then="NATO bombing in August and September and the Dayton Accords of November 1995 ended the war, dividing Bosnia into two largely self-governing entities. The International Court of Justice ruled the massacre genocide in 2007.",
 fact="The Dutch government resigned in 2002 after a report on its peacekeepers' failure at Srebrenica."),
"eu2002114": dict(
 bluf="Twelve EU governments replaced their national currencies with one money run by the European Central Bank, to complete the single market and to bind their states together. The changeover went smoothly, and about 350 million people now use the euro in 21 countries. The currency eased trade and locked very different economies into one monetary policy without a shared budget, the flaw the debt crisis of 2010 exposed.",
 now="One interest rate for economies as different as Germany's and Greece's, with no shared budget, is the mechanism behind the 2010 debt crisis and the reason the eurozone built a rescue fund and a banking union after it; 21 countries use the euro since Bulgaria joined in 2026.",
 story="On 1 January 2002 euro notes and coins entered circulation in 12 countries, replacing the mark, franc, lira and nine other currencies; the euro had existed for banks and markets since 1999. The European Central Bank in Frankfurt set one interest rate for all of them. The old currencies ceased to be legal tender within two months.",
 why="Europe's leaders wanted a single market with one money and a symbol of political unity. German Chancellor Helmut Kohl had accepted the currency at Maastricht as the price of a reunified Germany inside Europe.",
 then="Trade across the zone became easier. Cheap credit flowed to Greece, Ireland, Spain and Portugal; when it stopped in 2010 they could neither devalue nor borrow, and the eurozone improvised rescue mechanisms.",
 fact="The bridges and windows on euro notes are imaginary, so no country is favoured."),
"eu2004115": dict(
 bluf="The EU admitted ten states, eight of them from the former Soviet bloc, to anchor them in the West's political and economic system and to close the division of the Cold War. The enlargement, the largest in its history, raised the new members' growth and sent millions of their workers west. It also hardened the line between the EU and Russia, which treated the expansion as encroachment, and made the new members' fear of Russia a force in EU policy.",
 now="The new members' fear of Russia, carried into Brussels from 2004, shapes EU policy on Ukraine and sanctions; the migration the enlargement set off fed the Brexit vote of 2016.",
 story="On 1 May 2004 ten countries joined the European Union: Poland, the Czech Republic, Slovakia, Hungary, Slovenia, Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania, Cyprus and Malta. Eight had been in the Soviet bloc 15 years earlier. Britain, Ireland and Sweden opened their labour markets to the new members at once; most other states kept transition periods of up to seven years.",
 why="The EU wanted former Soviet-bloc states anchored in the West before Russia recovered. The candidates wanted the market, the money and the security that membership carried.",
 then="Millions moved west for work, especially to Britain, Ireland and Germany, which later fed the Brexit debate. Romania and Bulgaria joined in 2007 and Croatia in 2013. Russia treated the expansion as encroachment.",
 fact="Poland alone brought 38 million people, more than the other nine together."),
"eu2008116": dict(
 bluf="Georgian President Mikheil Saakashvili moved to retake the breakaway region of South Ossetia; Russia under Prime Minister Vladimir Putin and President Dmitry Medvedev went to war to stop him and to block Georgia's path into NATO. Russian forces won in five days and recognised South Ossetia and Abkhazia as independent. Georgia's NATO bid stalled, and Russian troops have garrisoned both regions since.",
 now="The war fixed the pattern of breakaway regions held by Russian troops that Moscow applied in Ukraine from 2014; one reading holds that the mild Western response encouraged those moves, the other that Russia acted on its own calculation of Ukraine's turn west.",
 story="After weeks of skirmishes, Georgian forces shelled Tskhinvali, capital of the breakaway region of South Ossetia, on the night of 7-8 August 2008. Russian forces entered through the Roki tunnel, pushed deep into Georgia within five days and bombed Gori and Poti. French President Nicolas Sarkozy brokered a ceasefire on 12 August. Russia recognised South Ossetia and Abkhazia as independent on 26 August.",
 why="Georgia's push to join NATO came first; the Bucharest summit of April 2008 had promised it membership without a date. Russia was determined to stop it, and the breakaway regions gave both sides the ground to fight on.",
 then="The West's response was limited to suspended talks and a ceasefire. Russian troops have stayed in both regions since, and Georgia's NATO bid stalled.",
 fact="An EU inquiry in 2009 found that Georgia fired first and that Russia's response went far beyond defence."),
"eu2010117": dict(
 bluf="Greece and then Ireland, Portugal, Spain and Cyprus faced bankruptcy inside a currency they could not devalue; the EU, the European Central Bank and the IMF lent to them to keep the euro whole and demanded austerity in return. The euro survived, at a cost of a quarter of Greek output and unemployment above 25 per cent. The crisis exposed the flaw of a single currency without shared budgets and fed anti-EU parties across the continent.",
 now="Anti-EU populism surged across Europe, and the eurozone built new tools, such as a permanent rescue fund and banking union, to prevent a repeat.",
 story="In late 2009 Greece's new government revealed that its deficits had been hidden and were far larger than reported. Bond markets panicked about Portugal, Ireland, Spain and Italy too. The EU and the IMF offered bailouts from May 2010 in exchange for austerity; Ireland followed in November 2010, Portugal in 2011 and Cyprus in 2013. European Central Bank President Mario Draghi pledged in July 2012 to do 'whatever it takes' to preserve the euro.",
 why="A shared currency without a shared budget left weak economies unable to devalue. Greece had hidden the true size of its deficits, and cheap credit in the 2000s had built up debts across the south.",
 then="Greek unemployment passed 25 per cent and the economy shrank by about a quarter. Draghi's 2012 pledge calmed markets. The eurozone created a permanent rescue fund, the European Stability Mechanism, in 2012 and a banking union from 2014.",
 fact="Greece needed three bailouts, in 2010, 2012 and 2015, and left the last programme in 2018."),
"d-euromaidan": dict(
 bluf="Ukrainians protested for three months to reverse President Viktor Yanukovych's turn away from an EU agreement and to end corruption; Yanukovych, with Moscow's backing, tried to hold on by force. More than 100 protesters were killed in February 2014 and Yanukovych fled to Russia. Ukraine's new government signed the EU agreement, Russia seized Crimea and backed separatists in the Donbas, and the war that escalated in 2022 began.",
 now="Ukrainians call it the Revolution of Dignity, the moment the country chose Europe over Moscow; Russia calls it a coup backed by the West against an elected president. Russia's seizure of Crimea and its backing of separatists in the Donbas followed within weeks, and the war that escalated in 2022 began there.",
 story="Protests began on Maidan Nezalezhnosti in Kyiv in November 2013 after President Viktor Yanukovych dropped an association agreement with the European Union under Russian pressure. They grew after police beat students on 30 November. On 18-20 February 2014 security forces and snipers killed more than 100 protesters. Yanukovych fled to Russia on 22 February, and parliament voted to remove him.",
 why="Anger at corruption came first. Yanukovych's sudden decision, under Russian pressure, to drop the EU agreement turned it into a movement, and the killings of February turned the movement against him.",
 then="Ukraine's new government signed the EU agreement in 2014. Russia seized Crimea in March and backed separatists in the Donbas from April; the fighting there killed about 14,000 people by 2021 (UN count, 2021). Petro Poroshenko was elected president in May 2014.",
 fact="The movement took its name from a hashtag, #euromaidan, used on its first night."),
"d-mh17": dict(
 bluf="Malaysia Airlines Flight 17 from Amsterdam to Kuala Lumpur was shot down over the Donbas by a Buk missile fired from territory held by Russian-backed separatists, killing all 298 aboard, 196 of them Dutch. Dutch-led investigators traced the launcher to a Russian army brigade; Russia denies responsibility and has offered alternative accounts. The deaths hardened EU sanctions on Russia and made the Netherlands one of Ukraine's firmest supporters.",
 now="Most of the victims were Dutch, and the case made the Netherlands one of Europe's firmest supporters of Ukraine; the dispute over blame, with Western courts and investigators assigning it to Russia and Russia rejecting their findings, is the pattern of the wider war.",
 story="On 17 July 2014 a Buk missile hit the Amsterdam to Kuala Lumpur flight over territory held by Russian-backed separatists near Hrabove, killing all 298 aboard. A Dutch-led joint investigation team found that the launcher came from Russia's 53rd anti-aircraft brigade and returned to Russia after the shooting. Russia's government rejected the finding and said the missile was Ukrainian or the evidence fabricated.",
 why="War in the Donbas came first, where Russian-backed separatists had received heavy anti-aircraft weapons from Russia and had been shooting down Ukrainian military planes. The flight was routed over the war zone at an altitude the airlines treated as safe.",
 then="The EU widened sanctions on Russia within two weeks. In November 2022 a Dutch court convicted two Russians and one Ukrainian of murder in their absence, and in 2025 the UN's aviation agency held Russia responsible. Russia rejects the verdicts.",
 fact="A memorial near Schiphol airport has 298 trees, one for each victim."),
"eu2014118": dict(
 bluf="Russian President Vladimir Putin moved troops into Crimea to keep the naval base at Sevastopol and to answer Ukraine's turn west after the Euromaidan revolution. Russia held a referendum under occupation and annexed the peninsula within a month, the first forcible annexation in Europe since 1945. Russia calls it a reunification endorsed by Crimea's people; Ukraine and most governments call it an illegal seizure, and Crimea's status sits at the centre of any settlement of the war.",
 now="The annexation was the prelude to the full-scale invasion of 2022; Crimea's status is central to any peace settlement, with Russia treating it as closed and Ukraine treating it as occupied territory.",
 story="After Ukraine's pro-EU protests ousted President Viktor Yanukovych on 22 February 2014, unmarked Russian troops took control of Crimea's airports and parliament from 27 February. A referendum on 16 March, held under occupation and recognised by few governments, reported 97 per cent for joining Russia. Russian President Vladimir Putin signed the annexation on 18 March. War broke out in the Donbas in April.",
 why="Putin feared Ukraine's turn to the West after the Euromaidan revolution, and the loss of Russia's naval base at Sevastopol. Russia holds that Crimea's Russian-speaking majority wanted to rejoin it; Ukraine holds that the vote was staged under occupation.",
 then="The West imposed sanctions and Russia was suspended from the G8. Russia backed separatists in eastern Ukraine, and the fighting there killed about 14,000 people by 2021 (UN count, 2021).",
 fact="The UN General Assembly declared the referendum invalid by 100 votes to 11, with 58 abstentions."),
"eu2015119": dict(
 bluf="More than a million refugees and migrants, most fleeing wars in Syria, Afghanistan and Iraq, crossed to Europe in one year, and EU governments split over whether to take them in or keep them out. German Chancellor Angela Merkel's government took about 890,000 asylum seekers; others built fences. The EU's asylum rules broke down, the EU paid Turkey to stop crossings, and migration became the most divisive issue in European politics.",
 now="Migration became the most divisive issue in European politics, and the EU adopted a new pact on migration and asylum in 2024.",
 story="In 2015 more than a million refugees and migrants reached Europe, most crossing from Turkey to Greek islands and walking north through the Balkans. Most were fleeing wars in Syria, Afghanistan and Iraq. German Chancellor Angela Merkel said on 31 August 'we can do this' and kept Germany's borders open; Germany took in about 890,000 asylum seekers that year. Hungary closed its Serbian border with a fence in September.",
 why="The Syrian civil war came first. Cuts in aid to refugee camps in Turkey, Lebanon and Jordan in 2015 pushed people on, and the open Balkan route showed that the journey could be made.",
 then="Border fences went up along the Balkan route. The EU paid Turkey 6 billion euros under a March 2016 deal to stop crossings. Germany's welcome gave way to tighter asylum rules, and the Alternative für Deutschland rose on the issue.",
 fact="The photograph of Alan Kurdi, a three-year-old Syrian found drowned on a Turkish beach on 2 September 2015, became the image of the crisis."),
"eu2016120": dict(
 bluf="Prime Minister David Cameron called a referendum to settle his party's argument over Europe and expected to win it; Leave campaigners, among them Boris Johnson and Nigel Farage, set out to take Britain out. Leave won by 52 to 48 per cent and Cameron resigned the next day. Britain became the first member to leave the EU, the union with Scotland and Northern Ireland came under strain, and the vote became the emblem of the revolt against globalisation.",
 now="Leavers hold that Britain recovered control of its laws, borders and trade policy; Remainers hold that it lost market access and influence for little gain, and polls since 2022 have shown more voters regretting the result than endorsing it. Relations with the EU are being rebuilt piece by piece.",
 story="In a referendum on 23 June 2016, called by Prime Minister David Cameron, 52 per cent of voters chose to leave the European Union on a turnout of 72 per cent. England and Wales voted Leave; Scotland, Northern Ireland and London voted Remain. Cameron resigned the next day.",
 why="Concerns over sovereignty and immigration came first, sharpened by the arrival of workers from the 2004 member states and the 2015 refugee crisis. Long-standing British ambivalence about Europe and discontent in regions that felt left behind did the rest.",
 then="Four years of political turmoil followed; Theresa May's government fell over the withdrawal terms and Boris Johnson won a majority in 2019. Britain left the EU on 31 January 2020. The pound fell about 10 per cent against the dollar in the days after the vote.",
 fact="The ballot question was changed before the vote from a Yes/No form to Remain/Leave on the Electoral Commission's advice."),
"d-vaccine": dict(
 bluf="Drug companies BioNTech and Pfizer, and Moderna, set out to make a vaccine against a new virus in under a year using mRNA technology that had never produced a licensed product, and governments paid in advance. Britain's regulator approved the first on 2 December 2020 and billions of doses followed. Deaths fell sharply where the vaccines were given, rich countries got them a year before poor ones, and mRNA became a platform for other diseases.",
 now="mRNA technology now promises vaccines and treatments for other diseases, and its pioneers Katalin Karikó and Drew Weissman won the 2023 Nobel Prize in medicine.",
 story="On 2 December 2020 Britain's medicines regulator, the MHRA, became the first in the world to approve the Pfizer-BioNTech mRNA vaccine, eleven months after the virus's genome was published. Margaret Keenan, aged 90, received the first shot in Coventry on 8 December. The United States approved it on 11 December and Moderna's vaccine a week later. Billions of doses followed worldwide.",
 why="Decades of mRNA research came first. Public funding and advance purchases removed the commercial risk, and regulators ran trials and reviews in parallel instead of in sequence.",
 then="Vaccines cut deaths sharply in 2021. Rich countries had vaccinated most adults by mid-2021 while much of Africa waited into 2022. Karikó and Weissman won the 2023 Nobel Prize in medicine.",
 fact="The second person vaccinated in Britain was an 81-year-old named William Shakespeare."),
"eu2020121": dict(
 bluf="Prime Minister Boris Johnson's government set out to take Britain out of the EU's single market and customs union on the terms of the 2016 vote, and the EU set out to protect its market and the open Irish border. Both got most of what they wanted: Britain left on 31 January 2020 with a trade deal at the end of the year, and Northern Ireland stayed inside EU goods rules. New barriers cut trade with Europe, and the two sides are still settling their relationship.",
 now="Leavers hold that Britain recovered control of its laws and trade policy; Remainers hold that the barriers cost trade and growth, and the government's budget forecaster put the long-run loss at about 4 per cent of output (2021 estimate). In 2025 the two sides agreed a new partnership on defence and trade.",
 story="After years of deadlock in Parliament, Prime Minister Boris Johnson won a large majority in December 2019 promising to 'get Brexit done'. The UK left the EU on 31 January 2020. A transition period ended on 31 December 2020 with a trade deal agreed on 24 December. The deal kept goods trade free of tariffs and brought customs checks, rules-of-origin paperwork and an end to free movement.",
 why="The 2016 referendum result came first. The 2019 election broke the deadlock in Parliament, and the EU's insistence on no hard border in Ireland shaped the Northern Ireland terms.",
 then="New customs checks hit trade with Europe. A special arrangement kept Northern Ireland inside EU goods rules, creating a trade border in the Irish Sea, which the Windsor Framework of 2023 eased.",
 fact="The trade deal was agreed on Christmas Eve 2020, a week before the deadline."),
"d-zeitenwende": dict(
 bluf="German Chancellor Olaf Scholz answered Russia's invasion of Ukraine by ending the settlement under which reunified Germany had bought Russian gas, exported to China and left defence to the United States, and by rearming on a scale with no precedent since 1945. Germany met NATO's 2 per cent target in 2024 and committed to 3.5 per cent by 2035. Supporters hold that it restores Europe's capacity to defend itself; opponents hold that it diverts money from welfare and reopens the question of German power that 1990 was meant to close.",
 now="Every neighbour is recalculating: Poland and the Baltic states want a stronger Germany, France wants it inside European structures, and the German public is divided about whether it wants it at all.",
 story="On 27 February 2022 German Chancellor Olaf Scholz told the Bundestag that Russia's invasion was a Zeitenwende, a turning of the times, and announced a 100 billion euro special fund for the Bundeswehr, a commitment to spend 2 per cent of output on defence, and the end of Nord Stream 2. Germany reached the 2 per cent mark in 2024. In March 2025 the Bundestag amended the constitutional debt brake to exempt defence spending above 1 per cent of output and created a 500 billion euro infrastructure fund, and in June 2025 Germany under Chancellor Friedrich Merz accepted NATO's new target of 3.5 per cent for core defence by 2035.",
 why="Thirty years of the peace dividend had cut the Bundeswehr to a force short of ammunition and left German industry dependent on Russian gas; the invasion made both into liabilities within a week. American pressure on Europe to carry its own defence had been rising since 2016.",
 trig="Scholz's Bundestag speech of 27 February 2022 announced the 100 billion euro fund and the 2 per cent commitment; the debt brake was amended for defence in March 2025.",
 then="Germany met the 2 per cent target in 2024, committed to 3.5 per cent by 2035 and began building the largest army in Western Europe. Parties on the far right and the left voted against the debt-brake change, and the question of German power that 1990 had been meant to close is open again.",
 fact="The 100 billion euro fund was double Germany's regular defence budget of about 50 billion euros in 2022."),
"eu2022122": dict(
 bluf="Russian President Vladimir Putin invaded Ukraine to remove President Volodymyr Zelensky's government and to stop Ukraine's turn to the West; Ukraine fought to survive as an independent state. Kyiv held, Russia took and holds about a fifth of the country (official estimates, 2024), and the war became the largest in Europe since 1945. It ended the post-Cold War order in Europe, brought Finland and Sweden into NATO, set off rearmament and drew Russia toward China, Iran and North Korea.",
 now="Russia holds that it acted to protect Russian speakers, block NATO and reverse a Western-backed coup of 2014; Ukraine and its allies hold that it is a war of conquest against a sovereign state. The outcome will set the credibility of borders in Europe and the future of US alliances.",
 story="On 24 February 2022 Russian forces invaded from the north, east and south, expecting to take Kyiv in days. Ukrainian forces under President Volodymyr Zelensky held the capital, and Russia withdrew from the north in April and from Kherson in November 2022. The war turned into grinding battles in the east and south, fought with drones, artillery and trenches.",
 why="Putin rejects Ukraine's independent, Western-oriented path and has called Russians and Ukrainians 'one people'. He portrays NATO's eastward expansion as a threat. Ukraine's government and most of its people chose the West after 2014 and refused the neutrality Moscow demanded.",
 trig="The invasion of 24 February 2022, after months of military build-up and Russian demands that NATO pledge never to admit Ukraine.",
 then="Finland joined NATO in 2023 and Sweden in 2024. Europe cut its Russian gas imports and raised defence spending. Casualties run to hundreds of thousands killed and wounded on each side (Western official estimates, 2024), and more than 6 million Ukrainians were refugees abroad (UN count, 2024).",
 fact="US officials offered Zelensky evacuation in the first days; his reported reply was that he needed ammunition rather than a ride."),
"d-wagner": dict(
 bluf="Mercenary chief Yevgeny Prigozhin marched his Wagner Group on Moscow to force out Defence Minister Sergei Shoigu and Chief of Staff Valery Gerasimov after months of feuding over the war in Ukraine. His fighters took Rostov-on-Don and reached within about 200 km of Moscow before a deal brokered by Belarusian President Alexander Lukashenko stopped them. Russian President Vladimir Putin's rule survived its most serious challenge in two decades; Prigozhin died in a plane crash two months later and Wagner was broken up.",
 now="The mutiny showed that Russian President Vladimir Putin's control rested in part on armed men outside the army's chain of command, and his answer, Prigozhin's death and Wagner's absorption by the defence ministry, showed how he closes such gaps.",
 story="On 23 June 2023 Wagner chief Yevgeny Prigozhin accused the defence ministry of attacking his camps and sent his fighters into Rostov-on-Don, where they took the southern military headquarters without a fight. A column advanced up the M4 motorway to within about 200 km of Moscow, shooting down several aircraft on the way, before Prigozhin accepted a deal brokered by Belarusian President Alexander Lukashenko on 24 June. On 23 August his plane crashed north of Moscow, killing him and nine others.",
 why="A feud with Defence Minister Sergei Shoigu over ammunition, credit for Bakhmut and an order that Wagner's fighters sign contracts with the ministry by 1 July came first. Prigozhin had built a public following by attacking the generals' conduct of the war.",
 then="Wagner's fighters were offered contracts with the army, exile in Belarus or a return home. Its African operations were brought under defence-ministry control. Prigozhin died in a plane crash on 23 August 2023; Western governments said the Kremlin had him killed, and the Kremlin called the claim a lie.",
 fact="Putin called the mutiny treason on the morning of 24 June and dropped the charges against the mutineers by evening."),
}
